#{[17592186381249 :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))}{x+y}+1"] [17592186381250 :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x+y)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)}"] [17592186381286 :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain and the constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})) & =(2x)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x{y}^{2})+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(\\pi /4,1)} & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {1}^{2})+{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}+4\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186385185 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q}\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=\\mathbf{v}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =\\mathbf{v}{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(bt))(\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{\\text{x}}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is $\\text{unbounded},$ representing $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$ Thus, we must have $a\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{infinity}.$\n\nHence, to insure that ${e}^{at}\\to \\infty ,$ we require $a>0.$"] [17592186390978 :question/problem "$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\mathbf{x}(t),\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}(t)={e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2?$"] [17592186390978 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}(\\operatorname{ln}2) & ={e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{4(\\operatorname{ln}2)}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+16\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 48\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 48\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(\\operatorname{ln}2) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 48\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2(4)+0(48) \\\\ 0(4)+4(48)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 192\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390985 :question/problem "$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\mathbf{x}(t),\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}(t)={e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3?$"] [17592186390985 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}(\\operatorname{ln}3) & ={e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}3)}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+9\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(\\operatorname{ln}3) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+0(-36) \\\\ 0\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+2(-36)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -72\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390992 :question/problem "$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\mathbf{x}(t),\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}(t)={e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3?$"] [17592186390992 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}(\\operatorname{ln}3) & ={e}^{-2(\\operatorname{ln}3)}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+3\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(\\operatorname{ln}3) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+0(6) \\\\ 0\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+1(6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186391202 :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$"] [17592186391203 :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$"] [17592186391408 :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}-2x,\\hspace{2em}{y}_{2}(x)=10x-5{x}^{3},\\hspace{2em}x\\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}}, which is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $\\mathbb{R}.$"] [17592186391408 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{3}-2x & 10x-5{x}^{3} \\\\ ({x}^{3}-2x)^{′} & (10x-5{x}^{3})^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{3}-2x & 10x-5{x}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2 & 10-15{x}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({x}^{3}-2x)(10-15{x}^{2})-(10x-5{x}^{3})(3{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-2)\\cdot 5(2-3{x}^{2})-5x(2-{x}^{2})(3{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =5x({x}^{2}-2)(2-3{x}^{2})-5x({x}^{2}-2)(2-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-15{x}^{5}+40{x}^{3}-20x-(-15{x}^{5}+40{x}^{3}-20x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $\\mathbb{R}.$"] [17592186391410 :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$"] [17592186391411 :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$"] [17592186391420 :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}x,\\hspace{2em}{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}x+1,\\hspace{2em}x\\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $\\mathbb{R}.$"] [17592186391420 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 \\\\ {(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′} & {(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡x-({cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $\\mathbb{R}.$"] [17592186391425 :answer/value "zero for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$"] [17592186391426 :answer/value "nonzero for at least one $x\\in \\mathbb{R}$"] [17592186391460 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q}\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=\\mathbf{v}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =\\mathbf{v}{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(bt))(\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{\\text{x}}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q})$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that the given curve is $\\text{bounded}$ (and closed), representing $\\text{an ellipse}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nBut we have that:\n\n- ${e}^{at}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty $ when $a>0,$ and\n\n- ${e}^{at}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty $ when $a<0.$\n\nThus, we must have $a=0.$"] [17592186396571 :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}(xy)-y) &=\\operatorname{cos}(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}(xy)-1\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}(xy)) &=x\\operatorname{cos}(xy)-{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}(xy)\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}(xy)) &=\\operatorname{cos}(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}(xy)=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$  gives an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''III only''."] [17592186396573 :answer/value "III only"] [17592186398445 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(2)=-1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}2})={\\color{blue}-1}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}-1} & =\\frac{A}{{{\\color{red}2}}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{A}{4} \\\\ A & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"] [17592186398452 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(1)=1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}1})={\\color{blue}1}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}(A+x) \\\\ {\\color{blue}1} & =\\operatorname{ln}(A+{\\color{red}1}) \\\\ {e}^{1} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}(A+1)} \\\\ e & =A+1 \\\\ A & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e-1+x).\n$$"] [17592186398459 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y=\\frac{1}{A+x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}0})={\\color{blue}\\frac{1}{2}}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{A+x} \\\\ {\\color{blue}\\frac{1}{2}} & =\\frac{1}{A+{\\color{red}0}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{A} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2+x}.\n$$"] [17592186399657 :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xy.\n$$"] [17592186399657 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xy \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & ={x}^{3}{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{{y}^{-1}}{x}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}z=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-x\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-x) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}\\,.\n$$\n\nUse intervals with $x\\ne0$ and $C-x^3\\ne0$. The original equation also admits $y=0$, outside the requested nonzero family."] [17592186404482 :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{y}^{4}{e}^{2x})=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4) &=50{y}^{4}{e}^{x}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=10{y}^{5}{e}^{x}+4$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y) &=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}-1\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}) &=10{y}^{4}{e}^{2x}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2{y}^{5}{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-x}$ gives an exact equation."] [17592186436604 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡(3x-{y}^{2}),$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(0,\\sqrt{\\pi }).$"]}
